SET variant: predicate S = set-based reading; parent T = single-q tail predicate. T => S (T entails S). At the class level, the class conclusion is "no T exists" (parent) vs "no S exists" (variant). If T => S, then not-exists-S => not-exists-T, i.e., C_S => C_T. So C_T is weaker (implied by C_S), C_S is stronger. So the variant's class-level conclusion (no S) is STRONGER than the parent's (no T). Hence the variant SET is "strictly STRONGER" at the class/conclusion level. But at the predicate level SET is strictly weaker (T=>S means S is a weaker predicate; "no S" is stronger). My audit found the registry/delta strength head saying "strictly weaker than AF-WCC-VAC-GEN" is a class-level inversion, because a reader takes "variant X is strictly weaker than parent" as a claim about the class conclusion.

